  "narrative": {
    "intro": "This report summarizes federal contract obligations in Alaska for FY2026 to date. Total obligations to Alaska are $3.4B across 9,488 awards, with an average award size of $359K. Obligations fell 11.5% year over year. Fiscal trends show growth through FY25 followed by a partial-year pullback in FY26: FY21 obligations were $2.3B, FY22 were $3.3B, FY23 were $3.8B, FY24 were $3.8B, and FY25 were $3.9B. Buying activity is concentrated in a few agencies and industries. The Department of Defense obligated $1.4B, the Department of Homeland Security obligated $529M, and the General Services Administration obligated $496M. Construction and health-related contracting lead industries, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction obligations at $972M and Pharmacy Benefit Management obligations at $288M. Top vendors include DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. at $470M and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $288M. All totals are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Alaska changed this year?",
        "body": "Total federal obligations in Alaska are $3.4B for FY2026 to date, and obligations fell 11.5% year over year. Award activity to date includes 9,488 awards with an average award size of $359K. Historical obligations show a rise from $2.3B in FY21 to $3.9B in FY25, followed by the FY26 partial-year total of $3.4B. The current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate Alaska contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $1.4B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $529M and the General Services Administration obligated $496M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry at $972M, followed by Pharmacy Benefit Management at $288M and Facilities Support Services at $229M."
      }
    ]
  },
